readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|ulty, besides other meanings. But ''volition'' always signifies the act of willing, and nothing else. Willingness, I think, is opposed to unwillingness or aversion. A man is willing to do what he has no aversion to do, or what he has some desire to do, though perhaps he has not the opportunity; and I think this is never called ''volition''.
{{right|''Reid'', Letter to Dr. J. Gregory (Works, ed. Hamilton, p. 79).}}}}
{{smaller block|An artist’s brain receives and stores images often without distinct ''volition''. {{right|''T. Winthrop'', Cecil Dreeme, xvii.}}}}
{{smaller block|When a man’s arm is raised in sequence to that state of consciousness we call a ''volition'', the ''volition'' is not the immediate cause of the elevation of the arm.
{{right|''Huxley'', in Nineteenth Century, XXI. 495.}}}}
'''2.''' The power of willing; will.
{{smaller block|In that young bosom are often stirring passions as strong as our own, . . . a ''volition'' not less supreme. {{right|''D’Israeli.''}}}}
{{smaller block|The play of the features, the vocal exclamations, the gesticulations of the arms, &c., come under the domain of our ''volition''. {{right|''A. Bain'', Emotions and Will, p. 362.}}}}

¶
'''volitional''' (vọ̄-lish′on-al), ''a.'' [< ''volition'' + ''-al''.] Of or pertaining to volition.
{{smaller block|The ''volitional'' impulse. {{right|''Bacon.''}}}}
{{smaller block|What is termed self-control, prudential restraint, moral strength, consists in the intellectual permanency of the ''volitional'' element of our feelings.
{{right|''A. Bain'', Emotions and Will, p. 21.}}}}
{{smaller block|There is no more miserable human being than one in whom nothing is habitual but indecision, and for whom the lighting of every cigar, the drinking of every cup, the time of rising and going to bed every day, and the beginning of every bit of work, are subjects of express ''volitional'' deliberation. {{right|''W. James'', Prin. of Psychol., I. 122.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Volitional insanity''', a form of mental disease in which the most striking phenomena are those relating to per-}}

readings.c1s3:
verted or impaired will-power.

¶
'''volitionally''' (vọ̄-lish′o̤n-al-i), ''adv.'' In a volitional manner; as respects volition; by the act of willing.
{{smaller block|It was able to move its right leg ''volitionally'' in all directions.
{{right|''Lancet'', 1890, I. 1415.}}}}

'''volitionary''' (vọ̄-lish′o̤n-ạ̄-ri), ''a.'' [< ''volition'' + ''-ary.''] Volitional.
{{smaller block|Dr. Berry Haycraft gave an account of some experiments which extend our knowledge of ''volitionary'' movement and explain the production of the muscle and heart sounds.
{{right|''Nature'', XLI. 358.}}}}

'''volitionless''' (vọ̄-lish′o̤n-les), ''a.'' [< ''volition'' + ''-less.''] Without volition.
{{smaller block|The ''volitionless'' will.
{{right|''J. Owen'', Evenings with Skeptics, II. 415.}}}}

'''volitive''' (vol′i-tiv), ''a.'' [< ''volit(ion)'' + ''-ive.''] '''1.''' Having the power to will; exercising volition.
{{smaller block|It is, therefore, an unreasonable conceit to think that God will continue an active, vital, intellective, ''volitive'' nature, form, power, force, inclination, in a noble substance, which shall use none of these for many hundred or thousand years, and so continue them in vain.
{{right|''Baxter'', Dying Thoughts.}}}}
'''2.''' Originating in the will.
{{smaller block|Wundt regards apperception as a particular process, inserted between perception and ''volitive'' excitement.
{{right|''Alien. and Neurol.'' (trans.), VI. 519.}}}}
'''3.''' In ''rhet.'', expressing a wish or permission: as, a ''volitive'' proposition.

'''Volitores''' (vol-i-tō′rēz), ''n. pl.'' [NL., prop. *''Volatores'', pl. of ''volator'', a flier: see ''volator.''] In Owen’s classification, those birds which move solely or chiefly by flight, or are preëminent in ability to fly. {{smaller|It is the fifth order of the system, em-}}

